WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:01.320
WARNING!

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:04.919
This site is for SCP Personnel with 05 Approval.

00:00:04.919 --> 00:00:09.120
Access beyond this point for those with standard
Level 4 security is prohibited, and may result

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.620
in termination of Foundation employment.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:15.650
Unauthorized visitors who survived the memetic
kill agent are detained and interrogated under

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:17.800
truth-exacting memetic agents.

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:21.039
Dr. Herman Wright was used to high security
protocols.

00:00:21.039 --> 00:00:26.020
He had been working with the SCP Foundation
for several years, and had dealt with specimens

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:28.480
both terrifying and valuable.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.470
But something about this site was different.

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:34.950
The security was tighter than he had ever
seen - and it all seemed a bit much for what

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:38.800
looked like a run of the mill warehouse in
Alexandria, Egypt.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.830
The street seemed normal - it was surrounded
by businesses selling food and clothes, and

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:47.050
none of the locals seemed to give it a second
glance.

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:51.720
But as soon as he was approved for access
and entered the warehouse, it was a very different

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:52.720
story.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:56.950
He looked into the cognitohazard testing screen
- designed to cause psychic damage to anyone

00:00:56.950 --> 00:01:01.660
who hadn’t been inoculated by the Foundation
- and entered the large bunker.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:06.680
Automated guns lined the walls, he could see
the outlines of trap doors below him, and

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:11.470
he was pretty sure the whole place was rigged
to blow if a large-scale assault hit.

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:16.390
The Foundation was taking no chance with whatever
they had found in Egypt.

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:18.610
What could they be keeping locked up below?

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:23.410
As Dr. Wright walked through the long tunnel
leading to a staircase, he could see a series

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:25.330
of rules marked on the wall.

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:28.320
“No open flames allowed within SCP-4001”

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:33.140
“No firearms or bladed weapons allowed within
SCP-4001”

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:39.940
“All writing utensils brought into SCP-4001
must be approved by a majority of the O5-Council”

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:45.690
“Violating these conditions could cause
a CK-class restructuring scenario or an XK-class

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:47.500
end of the world scenario”

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:53.850
Dr. Wright’s mind raced as he entered the
massive antechamber and descended the staircase,

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.470
but he wasn’t prepared for what he saw.

00:01:56.470 --> 00:02:02.080
It was a library - the biggest library he
had ever seen, with bookcases stretched as

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.080
far as the eye could see.

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:08.810
The library was far bigger than the warehouse
looked from outside, and Dr. Wright assumed

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:13.060
it must be a disguise built on top of the
much bigger, older facility that had been

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:15.120
discovered by the Foundation.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.510
It wouldn’t be the first time the Foundation
found something that didn’t conform to the

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:24.790
laws of Euclidian geometry - SCP locations
often played by their own rules.

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:29.580
As Dr. Wright exited the staircase and walked
up to the first bookcase, he could swear he

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:32.459
heard rustling sounds among the shelves.

00:02:32.459 --> 00:02:33.940
Was something moving?

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:38.120
He hadn’t seen anyone walking the aisles
when he descended the staircase, and he was

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:42.050
pretty sure he was alone here - wasn’t he?

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:46.470
Making things stranger, a quick look at the
bookcase didn’t show any books he was familiar

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:47.470
with.

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:48.930
None had standard titles.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:54.880
In fact, they all seemed to follow a simple
format - each title a different individual

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:55.880
person.

00:02:55.880 --> 00:03:01.709
All the books looked exactly the same - same
binding, same thickness, same number of pages.

00:03:01.709 --> 00:03:04.599
Was this some sort of SCP reference library?

00:03:04.599 --> 00:03:09.090
Suddenly, a hand slapped down on Dr. Wright’s
shoulder and his heart nearly jumped out of

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:10.090
his chest.

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:14.900
He turned around to see a grey-haired man
in an SCP lab coat extending a hand to him.

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:16.180
“Dr. Waylon Henricks.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:19.610
Chief scientist in charge of testing at SCP-4001.

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:22.070
You must be Dr. Wright.”

00:03:22.070 --> 00:03:25.220
He didn’t even give Dr. Wright a chance
to respond.

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:29.660
Dr. Henricks didn’t seem like the kind of
man to be very interested in what others had

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:30.660
to say.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:31.920
“Yes, yes.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:34.000
About time the Foundation sent me a new research
assistant.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.370
I suppose you have a lot of questions.”

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:40.740
Dr. Wright was supposed to be a researcher,
not an assistant.

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:43.940
He didn’t suppose it would do any good to
point that out.

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:47.820
When Dr. Wright tried to question what it
was that he’d be researching, Dr Henrick’s

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:51.099
told him that it would be easier to show than
explain.

00:03:51.099 --> 00:03:55.709
With that he motioned Dr. Wright to a nearby
table, where one of the countless identical

00:03:55.709 --> 00:03:57.960
books on the shelves was waiting.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:02.790
He nodded at the book, prompting the younger
Doctor to turn it over and read the title.

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:05.190
He couldn’t believe what he saw…

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:07.739
There on the front of the book was his name.

00:04:07.739 --> 00:04:08.950
Herman Wright.

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:14.019
Dr. Wright looked at his colleague in confusion,
and Dr. Henricks motioned for him to take

00:04:14.019 --> 00:04:15.019
a look.

00:04:15.019 --> 00:04:20.190
It was written in a language he had never
seen before but was somehow instantly recognizable

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:21.330
and understandable.

00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:24.250
And the book really was about him.

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:29.330
Every detail of his life had been written
down in this volume, starting with his birth,

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:31.800
containing details that no one but he knew.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:34.340
His whole life story was here.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:39.130
From his childhood to his education to his
eventual recruitment by the SCP foundation.

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:41.630
But the book wasn’t completed.

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:46.880
The last line that had been written described
his entry into the secured confines of SCP-4001

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:49.120
that very day.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:52.060
Dr. Henricks had a mad glint in his eye.

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:54.400
“Are you starting to understand?

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:56.030
One for each of us.

00:04:56.030 --> 00:05:00.190
Every person who has ever lived, roughly a
hundred and twenty billion people since the

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:04.650
dawn of man, their lives all written down
in these books.

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:09.800
Every time someone is born, their book appears
in this library, and their story gets written

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:10.940
as it happens.

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:15.340
The Foundation has been monitoring this site
since the 1800s, but it’s been operating

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:19.330
much longer than that - since the dawn of
recorded history.”

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:22.530
Dr. Henricks passed Dr. Wright another book.

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:27.740
It was simply titled “The Hunter” and
it chronicled the life of a prehistoric man

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:30.860
who spent his days searching for wild game.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:35.400
He lived twenty-three violent, monotonous
years before his story ended abruptly when

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:40.320
a Sabre-Toothed Tiger decided it was interested
in the same mammoth he was hunting.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.100
This must have been one of the oldest books
in the library.

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:45.600
A book for every person on Earth.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:50.030
Dr. Wright could only imagine what the scale
of this massive library must be.

00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:55.330
Dr. Henricks explained that mapping the library
was a huge challenge before producing a map

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:57.840
from his pocket and laying it out on the desk.

00:05:57.840 --> 00:05:59.810
“You’re at base camp.

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:04.300
This is where new births generate on the shelves,
along with books pertaining to the first humans.

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:08.420
If you want to track down specific books,
you’ll need to know where you’re going.

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:12.010
We’ve established base camps around the
library, to mark significant eras in human

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:14.100
history with notable books.

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:18.090
At each camp, you’ll find generators, supplies,
and beacons to light your way.

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:22.100
If you’re going back far enough, you could
be traveling for days or even weeks.”

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:27.680
Dr. Wright was fascinated by this bizarre
location, containing the sum total of human

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:30.639
experience within its massively expanding
walls.

00:06:30.639 --> 00:06:33.669
But there was one thing he couldn’t figure
out.

00:06:33.669 --> 00:06:38.040
Just why was this seemingly harmless location
featuring constantly changing books the most

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:41.110
securely guarded SCP he had ever encountered.

00:06:41.110 --> 00:06:46.050
Dr. Henricks knew he was referring to the
extensive rules he must have seen as he entered.

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:52.270
The elder doctor reached into his pocket and
said “let’s just say that around here…

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:55.630
the pen is mightier than the sword.”

00:06:55.630 --> 00:07:00.069
He pressed the pen into Dr. Wright’s hand
and flipped open the back page of his book.

00:07:00.069 --> 00:07:03.830
It was blank, the rest of his story waiting
to be written.

00:07:03.830 --> 00:07:07.669
“What’s something you’ve always wanted
in your life, Dr. Wright?

00:07:07.669 --> 00:07:09.319
Think literally.

00:07:09.319 --> 00:07:13.520
Why not put it in your book, or remove something
you always wanted gone?”

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:18.520
Slowly, Dr. Wright thought back to his teenage
years and recalled an injury that he suffered

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:23.120
in a mugging that damaged his leg and left
him with a limp, he nervously used the pen

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:27.270
to scribble out the words and sentences describing
the event.

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:31.509
After a few seconds he felt a headache and
suffered from a slight nosebleed, after that

00:07:31.509 --> 00:07:36.539
the limp and pain from the injury was gone,
and he could no longer recall the event.

00:07:36.539 --> 00:07:38.340
“You see, Wright?

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:41.350
That’s the secret of SCP-4001!”

00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:44.080
Dr. Henricks said with a mad gleam in his
eye.

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:49.539
“This library doesn’t just let us read
the history of humanity - it lets us write

00:07:49.539 --> 00:07:50.539
it!

00:07:50.539 --> 00:07:54.860
That’s why you’re here - to test the limit
of this power and see what this library can

00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:56.710
do for the Foundation.”

00:07:56.710 --> 00:08:00.830
Dr. Henricks soon provided Dr. Wright with
footage of some of the many tests conducted

00:08:00.830 --> 00:08:04.819
in the library, and what Dr. Wright saw amazed
him.

00:08:04.819 --> 00:08:09.639
The books could rewrite the laws of nature
with alarming speed, as some unfortunate D-class

00:08:09.639 --> 00:08:11.130
personnel found out.

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:15.569
D-0546, a surly-looking prisoner in his 30s,
was brought into a room with a full head of

00:08:15.569 --> 00:08:16.569
hair.

00:08:16.569 --> 00:08:20.819
The D-class personnel was instructed to scribble
“Lost all hair” into his book, and he

00:08:20.819 --> 00:08:25.130
soon started scratching his head as he rapidly
started shedding hair.

00:08:25.130 --> 00:08:28.940
After less than two minutes, the man was completely
bald.

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:35.449
D-0567, a young woman who had been brutally
injured when attacked by an escaped SCP, was

00:08:35.449 --> 00:08:39.879
bedridden and would never walk again, according
to all the Foundation doctors.

00:08:39.879 --> 00:08:44.920
As the D-class personnel used a pen to scribble
over the line in her book describing her injury,

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:49.410
Dr. Wright watched as she suffered a minor
nosebleed - and then sat up, getting out of

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:53.749
bed as if she had never been injured, and
no longer remembered being attacked.

00:08:53.749 --> 00:08:58.730
Dr. Claire Williams, a Foundation researcher
sick with cancer, wrote into the book about

00:08:58.730 --> 00:09:02.700
curing her symptoms, and then addressed the
camera explaining that this was her third

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:05.482
time altering her own book.

00:09:05.482 --> 00:09:10.480
Her Lymphoma symptoms had returned after seven
months and two years respectively, but it

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:14.699
seemed that as long as she continued to make
changes to her book, then she could keep the

00:09:14.699 --> 00:09:17.439
cancer at bay indefinitely.

00:09:17.439 --> 00:09:21.950
But not all the tests ended with positive
results, as Dr. Wright moved on to another

00:09:21.950 --> 00:09:25.760
selection of tapes labeled “Mortality tests”.

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:29.910
A D-class personnel who was given a fatal
dose of drugs and then had their death erased

00:09:29.910 --> 00:09:35.130
from their book 45 minutes later miraculously
returned to life, but showed significant cognitive

00:09:35.130 --> 00:09:36.970
deficits after.

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:40.920
Another D-class, an older woman killed in
a containment breach, had her death erased

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:44.050
from the book - but came back deeply disturbed.

00:09:44.050 --> 00:09:49.670
She survived fifteen days in containment,
repeating “Send me back, let me go” over

00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:52.500
and over again before committing suicide.

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:56.800
A D-class personnel killed twenty-eight days
prior was brought back by writing “Returned

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:01.829
back to life” in their book - but died again
just thirteen minutes later from a cerebral

00:10:01.829 --> 00:10:03.189
hemorrhage.

00:10:03.189 --> 00:10:06.160
But it was the next tape that was the most
disturbing.

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:10.239
It documented a new book that was written
by the Foundation, depicting the life of a

00:10:10.239 --> 00:10:12.769
fictional man down to the slightest detail.

00:10:12.769 --> 00:10:17.089
It was carefully placed in the correct position
in the archives, resembling all the other

00:10:17.089 --> 00:10:21.799
books - and Dr. Wright watched the tape in
amazement as a man resembling the fictional

00:10:21.799 --> 00:10:27.600
character created for the book spontaneously
generated in SCP-4001.

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:32.880
Almost immediately, the man started vomiting
blood and he died less than two minutes later.

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:36.949
The book carefully described all the things
he died from, and stated that this was exactly

00:10:36.949 --> 00:10:39.160
as it was supposed to be.

00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:44.230
The fake book then disappeared into the archives,
never to be seen again.

00:10:44.230 --> 00:10:48.299
The archive knew when it was being used to
play with life and death - and it didn’t

00:10:48.299 --> 00:10:49.790
want any part of it.

00:10:49.790 --> 00:10:52.040
The next tape wasn’t a test at all.

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:56.480
It was a video interview, showing a Greek-speaking
scientist speaking with an ancient-looking

00:10:56.480 --> 00:11:01.910
old-man, found living in the archives shortly
after its discovery by the foundation.

00:11:01.910 --> 00:11:06.880
The man, who described himself as the Watcher
of Alexandria Eternal, believed he was keeping

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:10.529
the archives safe from the Roman empire and
other invaders.

00:11:10.529 --> 00:11:14.470
He was unaware that thousands of years had
passed since his watch began.

00:11:14.470 --> 00:11:18.939
He had been using the books to write himself
another day of life and to cure his ills ever

00:11:18.939 --> 00:11:21.179
since he began guarding the library.

00:11:21.179 --> 00:11:26.329
At the conclusion of the interview, he asked
to leave and this request was granted by the

00:11:26.329 --> 00:11:27.329
Foundation.

00:11:27.329 --> 00:11:31.180
The ancient man died of old age shortly after
setting foot out of the library.

00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:35.690
While the library can’t technically cure
death, it does seem to have nearly limitless

00:11:35.690 --> 00:11:41.170
ability to prolong life - as long as someone
is willing to stay close by and never forgets

00:11:41.170 --> 00:11:43.760
to write themselves another day.

00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:45.679
Where did this incredible power come from?

00:11:45.679 --> 00:11:52.220
An O5 authorized investigation into the origins
of SCP-4001 revealed that under the archives

00:11:52.220 --> 00:11:56.000
carpet, is a concrete floor covered by a layer
of ash.

00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:01.740
The ash was carbon dated to between 70 and
80,000 years old, and further analysis revealed

00:12:01.740 --> 00:12:06.800
the ash to likely be the remains of burnt
wood and paper, despite there being no known

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:11.299
records within the archive itself about a
major fire having occurred there.

00:12:11.299 --> 00:12:15.639
Dr. Wright didn’t have much time to dwell
on the library’s great power of life because

00:12:15.639 --> 00:12:19.110
he was suddenly shaken from his thoughts by
the sound of alarms.

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:21.059
Was the library being breached?

00:12:21.059 --> 00:12:26.740
No, it was Foundation officials, led by a
man he recognized as Dr. Lincoln Abrams - the

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:29.620
very man who assigned him to this project.

00:12:29.620 --> 00:12:31.459
And he did not look happy.

00:12:31.459 --> 00:12:36.209
He explained that he knew what Dr. Waylon
Henricks had been up to, writing in books,

00:12:36.209 --> 00:12:39.350
changing the lives of subordinates in an attempt
to play God.

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:44.129
He knew about everything, of course, because
he had read Dr. Henricks’ own book.

00:12:44.129 --> 00:12:46.399
He knew all of the doctor’s secrets.

00:12:46.399 --> 00:12:47.549
He knew everything.

00:12:47.549 --> 00:12:48.549
“No!

00:12:48.549 --> 00:12:52.529
I’m so close, Dr. Abrams - I know I can
crack the archive’s rules.

00:12:52.529 --> 00:12:55.660
There’s a way to conquer death here - I
can feel it!”

00:12:55.660 --> 00:13:00.420
Dr. Abrams was done listening, and with a
wave of his hand Foundation security took

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:03.749
Dr. Henricks into custody for a debriefing
and likely demotion.

00:13:03.749 --> 00:13:08.209
Dr. Abrams then turned his attention to the
new, young researcher.

00:13:08.209 --> 00:13:09.689
“Dr. Wright.

00:13:09.689 --> 00:13:11.259
Eventful first day, I gather.

00:13:11.259 --> 00:13:17.819
I’ll appoint a new lead scientist shortly,
but until then - don’t disrupt anything.

00:13:17.819 --> 00:13:21.370
Remember - the archive is writing our stories
as we speak.

00:13:21.370 --> 00:13:24.049
It’s not very happy with Dr. Henricks.

00:13:24.049 --> 00:13:26.769
Make sure your story turns out better.”

00:13:26.769 --> 00:13:32.079
And with that, Dr. Abrams and his security
team departed, leaving Dr. Herman Wright alone

00:13:32.079 --> 00:13:37.179
with the endless archives of Alexandria Eternal,
wondering what would be written in his own

00:13:37.179 --> 00:13:43.470
tome, as the sound of new books popping into
existence filled the air with ominous rustling.

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:48.540
For more on strange locations that defy the
laws of nature, check out “SCP-3008 - Trapped

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:50.610
in Ikea” or check out this video instead.

00:13:50.610 --> 00:13:51.610
But why stop there?

00:13:51.610 --> 00:13:52.610
